1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,570
Somewhere in a board meeting near you, someone is about to say the sentence that quietly disables more small nonprofits than any budget cut: we can't do advocacy, we're a 501(c)(3).

2
00:00:10,570 --> 00:00:12,555
This lesson unwinds that sentence.

3
00:00:12,555 --> 00:00:19,680
In the next five minutes you'll be able to name the four kinds of civic action, and know which of them you're free to use.

4
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:25,328
The cost of that sentence is years of pure service work while policy makes the problem worse.

5
00:00:25,328 --> 00:00:31,158
A literacy nonprofit watches reading minutes get cut, and never says a word to the school board.

6
00:00:31,158 --> 00:00:34,862
A food bank expands capacity while eligibility rules tighten.

7
00:00:34,862 --> 00:00:42,210
A youth program loses a third of its kids every June and never asks the city why summer funding hasn't moved in a decade.

8
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:48,387
The confusion has one source: advocacy is one word doing the work of four very different activities.

9
00:00:48,387 --> 00:00:54,008
Boards import worry from one category into the other three, and the fear disables all four.

10
00:00:54,008 --> 00:00:56,850
The fix is to separate them, so let's do that.

11
00:00:56,850 --> 00:00:58,420
Category one: education.

12
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,318
Category two: issue advocacy.

13
00:01:00,318 --> 00:01:01,954
Category three: lobbying.

14
00:01:01,954 --> 00:01:04,178
Category four: political activity.

15
00:01:04,178 --> 00:01:07,254
Three of these are green lights with no limits.

16
00:01:07,254 --> 00:01:11,507
One is allowed within limits you will almost certainly never hit.

17
00:01:11,507 --> 00:01:13,208
Exactly one is prohibited.

18
00:01:13,208 --> 00:01:18,901
If your mental picture is a wholesale ban, you've collapsed all four into the last one.

19
00:01:18,901 --> 00:01:20,340
They are not the same.

20
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:23,036
Education is teaching people about an issue.

21
00:01:23,036 --> 00:01:25,671
A fact sheet on the school funding formula.

22
00:01:25,671 --> 00:01:27,755
A community forum on housing data.

23
00:01:27,755 --> 00:01:30,696
A neutral training on what's actually in a bill.

24
00:01:30,696 --> 00:01:32,351
None of that is restricted.

25
00:01:32,351 --> 00:01:33,760
Do as much as you want.

26
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:38,592
Issue advocacy is taking a public position without tying it to a specific bill.

27
00:01:38,592 --> 00:01:41,283
Our community needs more affordable housing.

28
00:01:41,283 --> 00:01:43,729
Full-day kindergarten in every district.

29
00:01:43,729 --> 00:01:47,888
You can organize around those positions: petitions, op-eds, rallies.

30
00:01:47,888 --> 00:01:49,050
Still not lobbying.

31
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:59,525
Lobbying is narrower than everyday speech suggests: asking a legislator, or asking the public to ask a legislator, to support or oppose specific legislation.

32
00:01:59,525 --> 00:02:02,394
We need more housing funding: not lobbying.

33
00:02:02,394 --> 00:02:06,130
Tell your state senator to vote yes on HB 412: lobbying.

34
00:02:06,130 --> 00:02:08,732
Same idea, different sides of the line.

35
00:02:08,732 --> 00:02:18,340
And here's what almost no board has been told: 501(c)(3)s are allowed to lobby, within limits far above what a small nonprofit would ever spend.

36
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:24,169
Political activity is the one red category: supporting or opposing a candidate for office.

37
00:02:24,169 --> 00:02:24,816
That's it.

38
00:02:24,816 --> 00:02:26,241
That's the actual ban.

39
00:02:26,241 --> 00:02:29,220
Everything else you just heard is open to you.

40
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:32,352
So when the sentence comes up, name the category.

41
00:02:32,352 --> 00:02:35,611
Three green lights, one high ceiling, one red line.

42
00:02:35,611 --> 00:02:43,473
The full lesson, the reference card, and a board conversation script are in Academy, in Introduction to Nonprofit Advocacy.

43
00:02:43,473 --> 00:02:50,120
And when you're ready for the limits themselves, the next course is 501(c)(3) Advocacy: What You Can Do.
